// a list that already exists. But the operators aren't pre-split: each is a
// variable function — like [op] — recognized at the cursor, and only then is its
// precedence (declaration order) and direction known. So one pass *discovers* the
// triples; the rewrite rules then reduce them. Direction never gets computed here:
// it lives only in 188 (opl: right-to-left  m  opr: left-to-right).
// arity is always 1 (',' collapses many into one), so a bracket operator like {}
// is just a triple with a None side: prefix [None [{}] r], postfix [l [{}] None].
// atom | "(" expr ")" | block | None
// operator position: recognize the cursor as a variable-function operator.
// None => operand position / ⊣ : the list ends.  precedence = declaration order.
// deterministic — no forward-parse, no backtrack: () is an eager value carrying =>,
// juxtaposition (space) and ',' are operators like any other.
// an outer level owns it
// None if the line opens on a prefix op
// triple[i].r == triple[i+1].l (shared)
// <- 187 / 188 / 196..231, treating a None l/r as a bracketed side:
// ── Merged: 181/187/188/196/210 are one algorithm — "what to do with the loosest level" ──
// Every one of those rules answers a single question: at the loosest precedence band, do
// the peers SHARE an operand (chainable / opposite-direction -> expand with &, duplicating
// the shared operand), do they CONFLICT (mixed left/right assoc -> error), or is there ONE
// root (split, assoc = tie-break, which is also 187's right-fold)? So reduce is the keeper;
// 181/187/188/210 fold into its three branches; discovery (232) stays separate and feeds it.
// base case also covers brackets: [None [{}] r] => {}(r) , [l [{}] None] => (l){}
// the loosest operators (root band)
// (1) peers that SHARE an operand — chainable or opposite-direction (188): expand, duplicating it
// (2) genuine conflict (181 / 196 guard, but per-level: mixing across levels is fine)
// (3) one root, assoc is the tie-break (subsumes 187's right-fold)
// empty left  -> prefix bracket
// empty right -> postfix bracket
// The whole pipeline is then two functions, keeping the recognise/reduce seam:
// discover(cursor) => reduce(triples)   // recognition, may need scope/forward refs
// reduce(list)                          // pure fold: the only place precedence/assoc/direction/brackets decide
// NOT merged: discovery into reduce — that would re-entangle token recognition with tree shape.
// Hand-waves: `level.opposite_directions` is really 188's typed pattern
// (opl: right-to-left  m  opr: left-to-right); branch (1) is "shares an operand", and
// chainable vs opposite-direction are just two ways to detect that. The &-join relies on
// consecutive triples sharing the operand node (t[i].r == t[i+1].l) — exactly how discover builds them.
