// OR is parallel structure, AND is sequential structure

// From the journal, not yet reached here (spec). These are the language's
// own open questions, written where the language is written:
//
// `^` binds to the right.
//
// `@/` and `@./` name paths, so a word that starts with `@` is a location:
// `@https://…` is one, and a location with spaces in it is written `@" "`.
// `.` and `[]` stay reserved for domains, which is what keeps a typo like
// `.ra` from reading as a name.
//
// `->` and `,` are the same thing in most places.
//
// `:=` declares by initialising; `name: String = value` says the type too.
// The question is how a name being declared is told apart from one being
// written to, when the scopes are compatible.
//
// Comments nest: `/*` inside `/*` closes with its own `*/`.
//
// A character in quotes is a character, unless it is said to be a string.
//
// `[]` and `()` both make an array.
//
// `++` reads as `+` on numbers.
//
// A capture may ask for more than its text spells: `entry: String + Ray`
// keeps the Ray the entry is, where `entry: String` reads only the string.
// The Ray half is only there when it is asked for.
//
// A name may be typed by what it is: `var: class`, `var: class & String`.
//
// `PATTERN*` reads as `PATTERN, *`, so a path prefix is written `"PATH/"*`.
//
// A run of lines that each start with `.` is a block given to the value
// above it, so
//   a = obj
//     .first
//     .second
// leaves `a` as `obj`; writing `obj.` before the run calls them instead.
// spec: this is the leading-dot ambiguity in `Number.ray` and `String.ray`,
// where `x := y` followed by a `.member` line reads as `y.member`. What the
// journal asks for is that indentation decides, which is not what the `.`
// rule does today.
//
// Warnings the language should file: a `,` left at the end of a multi-line
// `()`, because either it is wanted or a newline is; and a first statement
// that begins with whitespace.
//
// Functions that never return.
//
// Right-to-left and left-to-right text in one expression.
//
// A function may be entered at a label, `func**.LABEL()`, and a branch may
// be written that is only reachable that way and never runs on its own.
//
// `error[X]` names the version the error type was introduced in, and a
// number within it.
//
// If a method is not on the value but the value casts to something that has
// it — `boolean` and `&`/`|` — the call converts and calls. When a library
// later defines that method itself, whoever relied on the cast is told.
//
// A superposed value holds where it came from, so a result can be refined:
// once one caller is known to be the one that happened, the result is what
// that caller made it.
//
// Types narrow by what was tested: inside `if array.length > 2` the array is
// the longer type, and `{== .instance_of IP}` casts. A function relaxes its
// arguments to what it actually used of them. Two dependent types that
// define each other are circular unless they share the condition that breaks
// it. `Ray<T = Ray>` says T is unknown but a Ray. Several edits that only
// together satisfy a type are one transaction.
//
// `assume` as a clause of its own.
//
// Every field is instantiated with `?()`, which is already the default; a
// field that has nothing is None.
//
// `ray[500..999] = …` has to say whether the range is given one value or
// each place in it is.
//
// A string may say what it is written in — `"".[LANG]` — and be highlighted
// as that.
//
// Subscripts and superscripts are written `v[…]` and `^[…]`, and the editor
// can put them back as plain names.
//
// A grammar read differently by two types is an error, not a preference.
//
// A pattern that says what may not follow does not succeed by stopping
// early: if the pattern does not match the whole of what it was given, it
// fails, unless something else in it matches the rest.
//
// An entry is its origin, not itself, and `.index` is counted from the
// origin.
//
// A signature per option, so each shape a rule accepts says its own:
//   ..{right: .} | {left: .}.. | .. (right?) =>
//
// And the questions the language is written to answer at all:
//
// Should two accesses of a variable that happen at once superpose? A range
// of values where the writes overlap would model what a language does.
//
// Is a function's control graph a function without a starting point?
//
// How is a function run backwards, and how are inverses of functions called
// and defined?
//
// What geometry describes a language for interfaces?
//
// How does a graph of a game scale to millions of players, and how is
// propagation through it limited by what there actually is to compute with?
//
// Something not filled in yet may still be called, lazily, on the promise
// that what goes there can only arrive after it is filled.
//
// Tests for the syntax itself, which change the external methods the grammar
// is written with and then read something.

// The draft's own project file (.ray2/.ray2.json) says what the language is,
// setting by setting. What it asks for and what is here:
//
// Reading. The text is read left to right, a file at a time: `Node.ray` first
// and then everything beside it, recursively, without reading that file
// twice. Here the entrypoint is read first and the project names the rest.
// The other direction is read too (`<-`), and mixing both in one expression
// is still open above.
//
// Structure is indentation, lines, and a newline between statements. A token
// runs until a space or a newline, and a word that is several words joined is
// split where it can be. spec: the splitting of compound words is not here —
// a word is what it spells.
//
// Brackets are `()`, `[]` and `{}`, and they nest inside themselves. A
// comment runs from `//` to the end of the line.
//
// A name is bound with `=`, aliased with `|`, and may be used before it is
// bound: what is unresolved is a forward reference. Scope is lexical and the
// receiver is `this`.
//
// Evaluation is lazy and runs to a fixpoint — the draft allows ten rounds;
// here the passes run until nothing changes.
//
// What binds tighter is what is written closer: precedence is positional and
// what is left associates first, unless a rule says otherwise.
//
// A pattern binds with `{name: type}`, offers alternatives with `|`, and
// takes its body after `=>`. A value is called by writing the argument beside
// it; calling what is not callable is an error, and what a value does not
// have itself it asks what it is made of.
//
// Diagnostics are collected rather than thrown, and each one says where it
// is. spec: the draft also asks for a repl, stepping through a program and
// browsing what it parsed to — that is the Ether's, not the language's.
